For eight years a polite bandit robbed stagecoaches in California and left poems behind. So how did they catch him? He stepped into the road with a shotgun and a flour sack over his head, took the Wells Fargo strongbox and walked away. He never shot anyone. 28 robberies. Twice he left a poem, signed Black Bart, the P08. Then in 1883, near Coporopolis, a robbery went wrong. He ran and dropped a handkerchief. On it was a tiny laundry mark, FX -07. Detectives checked about 90 laundries in San Francisco until one knew the mark. It belonged to Charles Bowles, a well -dressed gentleman. He got six years in San Quentin. After four, he walked out and vanished the way he had for eight years.
